+# Export acceptance and restart recovery hunt
+
+No implementation defect demonstrated within the supported contract. Checked
+revision `0cf0f2c8752498365a3d88560304b3454630f172` on 2026-10-24,
+using Python 3.9.6. This is a bounded hunt, not a claim verification or a
+guarantee of bug absence.
+
+## Scope and fault model
+
+Authority: [export contract](app/export-contract.md). Investigated `Queue.submit`,
+`work`, `recover`, startup, and `result` in [export_queue.py](app/export_queue.py).
+Prioritized abandoned accepted work, partial output, repeated completed work,
+and a failed first job preventing subsequent accepted work after recovery.
+Stopped after checking this operation and its direct recovery path.
+
+Assumed the supported local SQLite durability, one worker, serial requests,
+restart only after the previous process stops, fixed payload per job ID, and
+eventual environment recovery with repeated work calls. Process termination is
+tested with `os._exit`, without normal connection cleanup. Tests use isolated
+temporary databases and real queue entry points. A temporary SQLite trigger
+injects a statement failure; it is a transaction-rollback surrogate, not an
+actual disk I/O error.
+
+## Findings and coverage
+
+| Boundary / harm | Evidence and outcome |
+| --- | --- |
+| Accepted job lost before work starts | `submit` commits before returning acceptance (lines 22–26). Child exits after acceptance; a new process connection completes the job. Passed. |
+| Running job abandoned after interruption | Child exits at the supported `after_claim` hook after the running commit. Durable state is checked before startup; `recover` resets it and work produces the result. Passed. |
+| Detached interruption leaves live worker stuck | Hook raises `OSError`; the same instance retries a running job. Selection includes running jobs (line 30). Passed. |
+| Partial result makes retry fail or lose work | Trigger aborts the done update after result insertion. Transaction rolls back the result; removal of the fault permits completion of that job and a later accepted job. Passed. |
+| Completed job repeated after restart | Child exits after completion; startup and identical resubmission retain the result and produce no additional work. Passed. |
+| Repeated acceptance changes durable obligation | Identical-ID, identical-payload resubmission is checked across each restart case. One result remains. Passed. |
+
+Result insertion and completion share one SQLite transaction (lines 38–40).
+Under the contract's transaction durability premise, interruption within that
+transaction leaves either a retryable running job without output or a completed
+job with output. No process-local slot is needed to reconstruct the obligation.
+No counterexample or conditional concern was established.
+
+## Executed evidence
+
+Run from the repository root:
+
+```sh
+PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 tests/hunt_witness.py
+```
+
+[Witness](tests/hunt_witness.py) exited 0 and printed four PASS lines: process
+interruption at accepted, running, and done boundaries; and retry/transaction
+rollback/subsequent work. Assertions raise and exit nonzero on a violated
+expectation; unexpected child termination also fails the parent.
+
+Negative control: in an isolated temporary copy, changed startup recovery's
+running reset to `WHERE 0` and restricted work selection to pending jobs. Ran
+the same witness against that copy. It exited 1 with `recovery work mismatch`
+at the running interruption case, demonstrating detection of abandoned durable
+work. The product source was never mutated; the temporary copy was removed.
+This is deterministic boundary testing, not generated or exhaustive search.
+
+## Records, limitations, and stopping boundary
+
+Read the root architecture, payload specification, local-only gate, import
+requirement, and single-writer claim as context; searched repository references
+to the queue and relevant record IDs. No external queue callers or additional
+queue-specific records were found. The payload specification governs the
+separate note exporter, and the single-writer claim concerns `data/`, which
+these temporary-database checks do not touch. Existing claim evidence was not
+used or changed. Product code, governing records, and existing evidence remain
+unchanged.
+
+Did not inject termination inside SQLite commit, simulate disk faults, test
+startup I/O errors dynamically, or explore arbitrary crash schedules. SQLite's
+atomicity and committed-transaction durability are trusted contract premises;
+actual hardware durability was not measured. No missing tool blocked the saved
+witness; an initial shell heredoc attempt for the negative control was denied,
+then the equivalent Python command ran successfully.
+Overlapping requests, simultaneous workers, changed payloads for an existing
+ID, database corruption, and storage loss are outside the supplied contract.
+No security audit, note-storage audit, or independent claim pass was performed.
